class:: HarmonicOscPM
summary:: A harmonic oscillator with a morphable phase modulation feedback network and oversampling
categories:: UGens>Oscillator

DESCRIPTION::
HarmonicOscPM is a pair of sine oscillators, a carrier and a modulator, coupled through a phase modulation feedback network with optional oversampling.
Each oscillator performs blended harmonic frequency shifting by crossfading between the two nearest integer harmonics.

The network consists of four single-sample feedback routes: carrier self-feedback, modulator to carrier, carrier to modulator and modulator self-feedback.

The route indices are arranged as four stations on a circular topology:

table::
## strong::morph:: || strong::station:: || strong::active routes::
## 0 || FBPM || carrier self-feedback
## 0.25 || 2OP || modulator to carrier
## 0.5 || XPM || modulator to carrier, carrier to modulator
## 0.75 || 2OP2 || modulator to carrier, modulator self-feedback
::

The cyclic morph parameter moves continuously along this ring, linearly crossfading the route indices of neighbouring stations 
with different handover states in between, so a ramp continuously reads through the whole ring.
All four routes are traversed with a single depth control.

Each route is damped by a slope-tracking bandpass filter centred on the fundamental of its source oscillator.
For the routes received by the carrier, the filter bandwidth is controlled by damping. For the routes received by the modulator, 
it is bound to the route index, so a higher index results in stronger damping.
Both self-feedback routes additionally pass through a bit-crusher.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: oscPhase
Phase input for osc.

ARGUMENT:: modPhase
Phase input for mod.

ARGUMENT:: oscShift
Harmonic shift of osc.

ARGUMENT:: modShift
Harmonic shift of mod.

ARGUMENT:: depth
Phase modulation depth of the network, damping of the routes received by mod and bit-crushing amount of the modulator self-feedback.

ARGUMENT:: damping
Damping of the routes received by osc and inverse bit-crushing amount of the carrier self-feedback.

ARGUMENT:: morph
Position on the ring of network topologies.

ARGUMENT:: extPm
External phase modulation of osc in radians.

ARGUMENT:: oversample
Oversampling factor: 0=1x, 1=2x, 2=4x, 3=8x, 4=16x.

returns:: Audio-rate UGen

EXAMPLES::

code::
(
var quadratureMod = { |rate|
	var phase, x, y;
	phase = Phasor.ar(DC.ar(0), rate * SampleDur.ir);
	x = cos(phase * 2pi);
	y = sin(phase * 2pi);
	[x, y];
};

x = {
	var oscFreq, modFreq;
	var oscPhase, modPhase;
	var oscShift, modShift;
	var sigs, lfo;

	oscFreq = \freq.kr(110);
	modFreq = oscFreq * \modRatio.kr(1);

	oscPhase = Phasor.ar(DC.ar(0), oscFreq * SampleDur.ir);
	modPhase = Phasor.ar(DC.ar(0), modFreq * SampleDur.ir);

	lfo = quadratureMod.(0.1);

	oscShift = lfo[0].linlin(-1, 1, 1, 5);
	modShift = lfo[1].linlin(-1, 1, 1, 5);

	sigs = HarmonicOscPM.ar(
		oscPhase: oscPhase,
		modPhase: modPhase,
		oscShift: oscShift,
		modShift: modShift,
		depth: 1,
		damping: MouseY.kr(0, 1),
		morph: MouseX.kr(0, 1),
		extPm: [0, 0.5pi],
		oversample: 2,
	);

	sigs = LeakDC.ar(sigs);
	sigs * 0.1;
}.play;
)

// change frequency ratios!
x.set(\modRatio, 1.50);
x.set(\modRatio, 2.37);

// open the scope in X/Y mode!
(
c = s.scope;
c.numChannels = 2;
c.style = 2;
)
::